<Base lang="en" title="Writing"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>The words arrive before the biography.</h1><p>Fiction, essays, and fragments that need not justify themselves at once live here. Chinese is the natural home of the originals; English pages carry context and a small number of carefully selected translations — not hurried machine versions of every piece.</p><div class="grid"><section class="card card-essays"><div><span class="number">ESSAYS</span><h3>100 Small Things About Toronto</h3><p>A published essay collection: one hundred small entries plus a set of Montreal notes, written with no outline — the recurring threads only became visible in hindsight.</p><div class="essay-list"><details><summary>Tiramisu Made of Gold</summary><div class="essay-body"><p>To be honest, I had passed through downtown Toronto so many times, but the first time I ever heard of a high-end supermarket like Eataly was from my landlady.</p><p>But what I never expected was that my first time stepping into one of Toronto's gourmet supermarkets would be after I had already graduated.</p><p>My landlady was a young woman, not much older than me, so I often called her 'sis.' Seeing me still struggling between Uber Eats and DoorDash every day after graduation, she finally couldn't take it anymore and said to me, "Come on, I'm taking you to a good place to show you the real Italy."</p><p>That "good place" was Eataly, located on Bloor Street, the luxury shopping strip.</p><p>The moment I walked through the giant glass doors, I was completely captivated by the scene before me. This wasn't a supermarket. It was clearly a huge, three-dimensional, living and breathing Italian food theme park!</p><p>The first floor was a coffee bar and dessert counter, the air filled with the rich aroma of espresso and baked goods. People stood chatting with small coffee cups in hand, and the display cases were filled with all sorts of pastries so exquisite they looked like works of art.</p><p>The second floor was another world entirely. Fresh cheeses and hams hung at dedicated counters, chefs in white uniforms were making fresh pasta on the spot, and next to them were shelves selling all kinds of oddly shaped olive oils, balsamic vinegars, and seasonings.</p><p>The place was bustling with energy and noise, and everyone's face was glowing with a love for food.</p><p>Following my landlady, I felt like a country bumpkin who had stumbled into high society, my eyes barely able to take it all in. Everything here was a completely different world from the budget supermarkets I went to as a student, the ones that prioritized large portions and full stomachs.</p><p>Finally, we stopped in front of the dessert cooler. Amidst all the gorgeous panna cottas and fruit tarts, I spotted it at a glance—the tiramisu.</p><p>It was in a small, transparent square box, lying quietly under the light of the cooler. You could clearly see the distinct layers of coffee-soaked ladyfingers and thick mascarpone cream, topped with a layer of cocoa powder as thick as velvet.</p><p>"That's the one!" the dessert-lover in me screamed internally.</p><p>I reached out, pulled open the cold glass door, and picked up the box of tiramisu. Just before I placed it in my shopping basket, I saw the price tag. $20.</p><p>Twenty dollars for a small box of tiramisu. That price was enough for a chicken and rice meal from the Kabab shop behind the school, a meal that could last me for two. My 'broke international student' DNA screamed a piercing alarm at that moment.</p><p>My landlady saw my hesitation and said with a smile, "Trust me, you won't regret it."</p><p>With the tragic heroism of someone deciding to 'splurge just for today,' I placed the box of tiramisu into the cart.</p><p>After paying, we found a small table to sit at. I took a small spoon and, ceremoniously, scooped up the first bite.</p><p>The moment the spoon sank in, I knew the money was well spent. It was incredibly soft, with almost no resistance. I brought it to my mouth.</p><p>In that instant, I felt my taste buds awaken. The rich, slightly bitter cocoa powder melted on the tip of my tongue first, followed by the cool, silky mascarpone cream with a faint hint of liqueur, perfectly sweet without being cloying. At the bottom were the ladyfingers, soaked in rich espresso, with a moist texture and a deep, coffee bitterness.</p><p>These flavors blended perfectly in my mouth, layered and progressive, complex yet harmonious.</p><p>I admit, it was truly delicious. So delicious that I felt every single cent of that $20 was worth it.</p><p>It was then that I understood. Graduation might not just mean starting to look for a job and pay rent. It also means you can finally, occasionally, willingly pay for something 'unnecessary' but beautiful.</p></div></details><details><summary>The Sandwich Longer Than My Arm</summary><div class="essay-body"><p>After the immense shock of the medium-sized Coke at Wendy's, the next thing to inspire in me a profound reverence for the concept of "North American Sizing" was the legendary "footlong" sub from Subway.</p><p>That afternoon, Ivy and I had just been liberated from a sleep-inducing accounting class and were discussing what to have for dinner.</p><p>"Syna, I'll treat you to dinner today," Ivy said, suddenly pulling a stack of papers from her backpack and waving them in front of me like a nouveau riche tycoon who had just struck it rich.</p><p>I leaned in to look. It was a stack of beautifully printed coupons, colorfully adorned with the logos and discount information of various fast-food chains.</p><p>"Where did you get these?" I asked curiously. In my world, coupons were something you usually had to actively seek out or snag on an app.</p><p>"Oh, they just came in the mail," she replied nonchalantly, as if it were as common as receiving a utility bill.</p><p>But why had I, living in the dorm, never received these in my mailbox? I felt like I had missed out on a billion dollars.</p><p>"Look at this!" Ivy said, pulling one out like she was presenting a treasure. "Subway, buy one footlong, get one free!"</p><p>I knew Subway; we have it back home, and its main selling point is being healthy. It sounded good, and a buy-one-get-one-free deal was a godsend for a frugal international student.</p><p>"Let's go!" I was immediately swept away by the word "free" and happily agreed.</p><p>At the Subway near the university, a peculiar smell of baked bread and various vegetables hit me. Ivy and I stood in line. While I was still studying the dense options on the menu, Ivy had already skillfully begun her order.</p><p>"A footlong, on Italian herbs and cheese bread. Teriyaki chicken, toasted. Provolone cheese," she rattled off, and then the staff member's gaze turned to me.</p><p>My turn. I instantly fell into the same panic as when I first ordered coffee. Which bread? Which meat? Which cheese? And that long row of colorful vegetables and sauces behind them! This wasn't ordering food; it was clearly a pop quiz!</p><p>"The... the same as hers, please." Faced with immense pressure of choice, I shamefully gave up.</p><p>Then, I watched as the employee pulled out something that looked like a baguette from the bread cabinet and sliced it open down the middle with a long knife.</p><p>My heart skipped a beat.</p><p>A footlong. That's 30.48 centimeters! It was almost longer than my forearm! I stared blankly as the employee stuffed that giant loaf with chicken and vegetables as if building a wall, finally squeezing on several lines of sauce. By the time it was wrapped in paper, it had become a heavy, thick, veritable "submarine."</p><p>When Ivy and I each sat down with a "submarine," I fully understood the sense of absurdity I had felt at Wendy's at the beginning of the semester.</p><p>I needed both hands just to hold the sandwich, feeling like a hamster clutching a giant ear of corn. I opened my mouth as wide as I could but could only manage to bite off a small corner of its cross-section. Meanwhile, Ivy next to me had already effortlessly demolished a quarter of hers, eating with great satisfaction.</p><p>In that moment, I finally had an epiphany. In North America, the concept of a "single serving" was probably not what I understood it to be. The "serving for one" here was designed for a 200-pound man.</p><p>That meal, I tried my best and still only managed to eat half of my "arm-length sandwich." I took the other half back to the dorm, heated it in the microwave the next day, and had it for lunch.</p><p>From then on, whenever I saw a coupon that said "Free," I would shudder. Because you never know what a heavy, sweet burden is hidden behind that "free" offer.</p><p>(Excuse me ????)</p></div></details><details><summary>Turns Out There Is Such a Thing as a Free Lunch</summary><div class="essay-body"><p>Besides being the dorm's gossip center, Karen was actually a hidden "cooking fanatic" just like me. But since her family is Hindu, her culinary habits were rich with South Asian flair.</p><p>At first, due to certain stereotypes, I kept my distance from all Indian food. I always thought of it as a mysterious dark cuisine made up of countless spices I couldn't name and mushy textures.</p><p>Until one time, Karen showed off her skills in the dorm kitchen. She made a curry with chickpeas, potatoes, and various spices, served with fragrant rice. I took a bite with the attitude of "I'll risk my life to accompany a friend," and was instantly conquered by the rich, layered flavors of the spices. It turned out that real Indian food was completely different from what I had imagined! From then on, I became a loyal fan of Karen's cooking.</p><p>"What's this?" One day, I received a screenshot of a poster from Karen on Instagram. The design was simple, with a few cartoon vegetables and the words "Community Kitchen: Learn to Cook Healthy Meals!"</p><p>"It's a free cooking class at school," Karen sent in a voice message, her tone filled with the excitement of discovering a treasure. "I asked around and heard that you can take the food you cook for free right on the spot!"</p><p>The word "free," like a bolt of lightning, struck me right in the heart. As a poor and greedy international student, what could be more attractive than "free food"?</p><p>So, on a sunny Wednesday afternoon, Karen and I, two "foodie partners in crime," cheerfully headed to the "free lunch" site in the basement cafeteria.</p><p>It was a large area cordoned off by square tables, already filled with students. There were blond, blue-eyed local students, as well as international students like us from all over the world. A young white guy in a tall chef's hat was smilingly handing out aprons and recipes to everyone.</p><p>Today's theme was—Vietnamese Spring Rolls.</p><p>Karen and I received our "battle supplies": translucent rice paper, fresh shrimp, thin rice noodles, and a large pile of refreshing-looking vegetables like lettuce, mint leaves, and shredded carrots.</p><p>The process was much more complicated than I had imagined. It wasn't just about chopping and cooking; it was more like a delicate craft class. The hardest part was handling the dry, brittle rice paper. The chef told us to soak it in warm water for a few seconds until it became soft and transparent, but not too soft, or it would stick together into an unsalvageable tragedy.</p><p>I carefully placed the first sheet of rice paper into the water, silently counting "one, two, three," then clumsily pulled it out. The result was either still hard on one side or already a mushy mess on the other. Karen, on the other hand, once again showed her "cooking fanatic" talent, handling every sheet of rice paper perfectly.</p><p>The kitchen was filled with exclamations and laughter in various languages. Someone would wail because their rice paper tore, while another would cheer for successfully rolling their first spring roll. It felt less like a class and more like a lively and challenging craft party.</p><p>When we carefully wrapped the cooked shrimp, rice noodles, and various shredded vegetables in the soft rice paper, a translucent spring roll, faintly revealing the colorful fillings inside, was born in our hands.</p><p>"Okay, everyone! Time to enjoy the meal you made!" the chef announced loudly.</p><p>Karen and I took our plate of incredibly professional-looking Vietnamese spring rolls, paired with a special peanut sauce, and found a seat by the window. I picked one up, dipped it in the sauce, and took the first bite. The rice paper was soft and chewy, the shrimp sweet, the vegetables refreshing, and the aroma of mint exploded in my mouth, all complemented by the rich peanut sauce…</p><p>It was so delicious!</p><p>I looked at the students coming and going outside the window, then at the substantial, delicious, and most importantly—completely free—lunch in my hands, my heart filled with a huge sense of satisfaction and happiness.</p><p>It was then that I understood that the saying my parents taught me since I was a child, "There's no such thing as a free lunch," didn't hold true at this magical Canadian university.</p><p>Here, there was not only a free lunch, but also free cooking lessons, and the priceless joy of sharing food with friends.</p></div></details><details><summary>My Taste Buds Are Saved!</summary><div class="essay-body"><p>If universities back home are backed by vibrant snack streets, then North American universities can only be described as "barren."</p><p>When I was still back home, the back alleys and snack streets of my university were my source of joy. There was steaming hot Malatang, sizzling Teppanyaki, fragrant grilled cold noodles, and the ever-popular Takoyaki with its perpetual queue. Every small stall was a portal to a comforting world of deliciousness.</p><p>But after arriving in Toronto, I discovered that the "cuisine" around the university was basically monopolized by a few fast-food giants. Tim Hortons, Subway, Pizza Pizza... they were like tireless copy-paste templates, occupying every prime location.</p><p>Eating them once or twice felt new, but after a solid week, I felt like my taste buds were being completely formatted.</p><p>Just when I thought my university life was destined to be a cycle of burgers, sandwiches, and donuts, I discovered the inconspicuous little street behind the university.</p><p>There were no bright signs of chain stores, only a few small, slightly worn-down storefronts. And the ones that left the deepest impression on me were the tiny sushi shop and the Kabab place next to it.</p><p>The sushi shop was so small that it only had one counter and two or three tables. The owners were a quiet Chinese couple. They didn't talk much and always wore gentle smiles. The husband was in charge of making sushi behind the counter, while the wife took orders and packed them.</p><p>Their movements weren't fast, but every step was filled with care. The first time I went, I ordered a simple salmon sushi. The owner took out a whole piece of fish from the cooler and, with a long knife, deftly sliced off a few pieces. The texture of the fish glistened freshly under the light.</p><p>He placed the fish slice on the warm rice and, with a gentle squeeze, a perfect piece of sushi was born. I put it in my mouth, and the richness of the fish and the slight tartness of the rice melted together. In that moment, I felt healed.</p><p>Later, that little sushi shop became my secret spot for treating myself. Oh, and besides the regular sushi, their poke bowls were also exceptional. Even after I graduated and moved to an off-campus apartment, I would still often order takeout from them on Uber Eats. It was a familiar comfort etched into my taste buds.</p><p>The Kabab shop next door, however, had a completely different vibe. The owner was an enthusiastic Middle Eastern man, always wearing an apron stained with sauce, who would greet every student loudly in his heavily accented English.</p><p>His shop was always filled with a powerful, rich aroma of grilled meat and various spices. Giant skewers of meat sizzled on the grill, the fat dripping onto the charcoal and sending up puffs of white smoke.</p><p>His shop was a blessing for all hungry students because it had one defining characteristic: huge portions, and cheap.</p><p>My usual order was the Chicken with Rice. For under twenty dollars (the price has probably gone up by now), the owner would give you a giant container piled high with golden spiced rice, topped with a mountain of grilled chicken, and served with a large portion of salad and golden-brown naan bread.</p><p>The portion was so substantial it could satisfy any big guy. Usually, I could split one dinner into two meals; reheating it in the microwave the next day made for another hearty lunch.</p><p>And so, one quiet, one lively; one delicate, one hearty. These two small shops, like two silent guardians, held up a space for us students, surrounded by bland campus food and fast food, to breathe—a world filled with human warmth and authentic flavors.</p><p>They were the most delicious and the brightest stars on my barren campus food map.</p></div></details></div></div></section><section class="card"><div><span class="number">FANFICTION</span><h3>Fan fiction</h3><p>Mostly Genshin Impact, centered on one pairing across roughly thirty pieces, plus a handful of short pieces for other works. Someone else's story, carrying my own real thinking underneath.</p></div></section></div><aside class="note">Private files and unselected drafts are never synced here, and nothing is published without being cleared first.</aside></article></Base>

